\title{Dotaro}
\date{}

\begin{document}
\small
\begin{center}
{\LARGE\bfseries Dotaro}
\end{center}
\vspace{2pt}

\begin{description}[nosep]
\item[Players] 3--9
\item[Equipment] One \emph{Dotaro Deck} (108 cards)
\item[Objective] Score the \emph{fewest} points across a round of hands
\item[License] \href{https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/}{CC-BY-4.0}
\end{description}

\section{Overview}

\emph{Dotaro}---named for \emph{Domino Tarot}---is a shedding game where you try to empty your hand of (high point) cards before your opponents do.
All players build a single chain together, extending either of its two open ends domino-style using tarot-style card rankings.
Fewest total points after a full round of hands wins the match.

\section{Setup}

Shuffle the full 108-card deck and deal 12 cards to each player. Any cards left over are set aside.

\section{The chain}

Whoever deals leads the hand by playing any one card from their hand, face up.
Its two halves become the chain's two open ends.
Play then passes around the table. On your turn, you may extend either open
end if you can beat it (see below). If you cannot legally beat either end,
you are skipped.

\section{Extending an end}

To extend an open end the \emph{light/dark of the extending half must match:} if your open end is light, then your extending half must also be light (and if your open end is dark, then your extending half must also be dark).
Additionally the extending card half must \emph{beat the open end:}

\begin{itemize}
\item \emph{Traditional-suit end:} beat it with a higher rank in the
      \emph{same} suit, with any number-suit half (number suits are
      trumps: any number-suit half beats any traditional-suit half), or
      with a fool half.
\item \emph{Number-suit end:} read the end as a two-digit number, suit as
      the tens digit and rank as the ones digit; beat it with a
      higher two-digit number, or with a fool half. A traditional-suit
      half can never beat a number-suit end.
\item \emph{Fool end:} beaten by anything of the matching color.
\end{itemize}

Whichever half of your card you used to extend is now buried; the
\emph{other} half of your played card becomes the new open end on that side.

\section{Ending a hand}

The hand ends as soon as either:

\begin{itemize}
\item \emph{Someone empties their hand}, or
\item \emph{The chain blocks}---a full circuit of the table passes with
      no successful play in between.
\end{itemize}

Either way, every player then scores their \emph{remaining} hand (see
``Scoring'').

\section{Scoring}

For each card remaining in your hand, add up the point value of both its
halves:

\begin{itemize}
\item Traditional-suit half: its rank value (1--10, or 11/12/13/14 for
      pawn\slash knight\slash queen\slash king).
\item Number-suit half: its two-digit value (suit as the tens digit, rank as
      the ones digit), 0--49.
\item Fool half: 0.
\end{itemize}

Your score for the hand is the sum over all your remaining cards. If you
went out, your hand is empty and you score zero.

\section{Playing a match}

\begin{itemize}
\item A match is one full round: each player deals and leads exactly one
      hand, then the round ends.
\item Points accumulate hand to hand across the round.
\item Whoever has the \emph{lowest} total after the round wins the match (ties are possible).
\end{itemize}

